{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794751353,"nonce":"YAYmtX8PBkANF7zSzw3Cf5ibG1o=","message":"The split of that CPU, measured (same scene, three fading blocks over 100 static cards, `/usr/bin/time -l` over 20 s, startup included in both runs): idle 0.16 s user + 0.11 s sys; animated 0.80 s user + 0.68 s sys. The animation adds 0.64 s user and 0.57 s sys, about 0.03 cores each, so roughly half of what a time-only effect costs on desktop is kernel and driver time (Metal submit, present, mach messages), which no change to the frame loop's own logic reaches. A 10 s `sample` of the animated window agrees: nearly every sample is a thread waiting; the running frames are a few dozen.\n\nSingle run each; the figure is a split, not a precise rate.","files":null}]}